      "prompt_tokens": 25,
      "completion_tokens": 961,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a transparent, tasteless, and odorless liquid that is essential for all known forms of life. It is composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, forming the chemical formula **H₂O**. Here’s a breakdown of its key characteristics and roles:\n\n1. **Physical States**:  \n   - **Liquid**: At room temperature (around 20–25°C), water is a liquid.  \n   - **Solid**: It freezes into ice at 0°C (32°F) and melts back into liquid when heated.  \n   - **Gas**: It evaporates into water vapor at 100°C (212°F) under standard atmospheric pressure.\n\n2. **Unique Properties**:  \n   - **High Specific Heat Capacity**: Water absorbs and releases heat slowly, helping regulate temperature in organisms and the environment.  \n   - **Universal Solvent**: It dissolves more substances than any other liquid, facilitating chemical reactions in living systems.  \n   - **Cohesion and Adhesion**: Water molecules stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion), enabling processes like capillary action in plants.  \n   - **Polarity**: The uneven distribution of electrons in H₂O molecules allows it to form hydrogen bonds, contributing to its high surface tension and other unique behaviors.\n\n3. **Role in Life**:  \n   - **Biological Processes**: Water is a medium for biochemical reactions, transports nutrients, and helps maintain cell structure.  \n   - **Hydration**: It makes up 50–70% of the human body, aiding in digestion, temperature regulation, and waste removal.  \n   - **Photosynthesis and Respiration**: Plants use water to convert sunlight into energy, while animals rely on it for cellular respiration.\n\n4. **Environmental Presence**:  \n   - Water covers about 71% of Earth’s surface (oceans, lakes, rivers) and exists in the atmosphere as vapor.  \n   - The **water cycle** (evaporation, condensation, precipitation) ensures its continuous movement through ecosystems.\n\nIn summary, water is a fundamental substance for life, with properties that make it indispensable for biological, chemical, and environmental processes. Its simplicity (H₂O) belies its complexity in supporting life on Earth."
